📝 Main Topics
1. AgentCore Baseline and External Integration Evaluation
Background:
The team expanded its initial LangSmith and LangFuse evaluation to include AgentCore because of the AWS-first direction. The comparison must distinguish native AgentCore capabilities from the incremental value of external integrations.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
The evaluation will begin with native AgentCore functionality covering runtime, memory, observability, and evaluations. The existing Freddie model and production behavior will remain unchanged wherever possible, and the working agent currently running on EKS will be deployed to the AgentCore runtime. LangSmith and LangFuse will be assessed only after the native baseline is established.
Responsibility and Actions/Follow-up Actions:
The team will assess AgentCore observability before deciding whether external tooling provides additional value.
The team will compare LangSmith and LangFuse integration capabilities with the native AgentCore baseline.
The team will examine AgentCore monitoring and tracing capabilities, including their relevance to evaluation and regression testing.
Native functionality will be tested before any external integration, consistent with the recommendation to try the native function first.
Unresolved Matters:
The final external integration product, deployment model, and detailed account-level implementation approach were not selected.
2. POC Account Ownership and AgentCore Environment
Background:
The POC needs an environment in which the team can own and manage the complete deployment, including AgentCore Gateway and AgentCore resources. The current production deployment on EKS does not, by itself, provide the required POC control model.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
The preferred target is a Freddy or otherwise controlled internal AWS account. The options include using an existing V2 AWS account owned by the team or requesting a new account without requiring a specific GPD account or Platinum setup. A new Freddy account was described as taking approximately three to four days after the request is initiated.
Responsibility and Actions/Follow-up Actions:
Hamid will work with Chris to obtain CTS alignment on the account and environment direction.
Chris will coordinate with Mike and the relevant teams, with a meeting involving Mike and the team arranged if needed.
The team will request guidance for establishing its own GPA/account and enabling the required capabilities.
Mike Ryan and the responsible platform contacts will provide the network and security information required for provisioning.
The team will follow up with Mike’s team, Rob Pietrich, or the appropriate owner regarding AgentCore availability and enablement.
Unresolved Matters:
AgentCore was not yet available in the relevant environment. The team still needs confirmation of its availability, enablement timing, whether it is limited to V2, and whether it is offered as an enterprise multi-tenant capability or as a separate service or account for each team. Network security, identity, governance boundaries, and the applicable identity-provider subscriptions also remain to be confirmed. Shared tenancy was not the expected model.
