4. Generic Knowledge-Base Use Case and Chatbot Demonstration
Background:
The team cannot immediately bring its SSGP code, training, and knowledge-base environment into the temporary workshop. A smaller use case is therefore required to demonstrate AgentCore behavior before the production POC environment is available.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
The initial prototype will be a single chatbot based on selected content from the Seller Services Guide or another generic public-data source. For each question, the chatbot will identify the relevant chapter and use the corresponding knowledge-base content to generate a response. The demonstration will focus on observability and evaluation rather than reproducing the complete production application.
Responsibility and Actions/Follow-up Actions:
The team will use the sources guide and related Seller Services Guide material as the knowledge base.
The team will select several chapters, break down and index the content, place it in a RAG setup, and run the LLMs.
Speaker1 will provide additional Seller Services Guide information if required.
The team will consider a LineChain knowledge-base application, a sample page, or the earlier Bedrock and solicit guide POC context where useful.
The team will evaluate runtime behavior, chapter retrieval, tracing, observability, and evaluation results with the limited chatbot.
Unresolved Matters:
The public-data or lab use case may not produce results identical to the Freddy Mac scenario because the temporary environment may use different models from the OpenAI model used through Freddy AI. The reuse of the earlier CGPT or Bedrock POC depends on whether the code and training can run in the selected environment.
5. Environment Dependencies and Deployment Governance
Background:
Deployment depends on account ownership, AgentCore service availability, platform approvals, and security boundaries. The team also needs a clear distinction between the temporary workshop path and the longer-term environment.
Key Conclusion/Resolution:
The team will seek a controlled AWS account rather than rely on the temporary lab for sustained work. Creating a new account requires alignment with the CTS team, CGS, and relevant CPS/CDS stakeholders. AgentCore enablement cannot simply be switched on as a marketplace capability; the required deployment and integration setup must be established manually.
Responsibility and Actions/Follow-up Actions:
The team will confirm whether an existing CTS, CPS, CDS, or other internal service can support the POC.
The relevant platform teams will clarify the AgentCore service and tenancy model before provisioning proceeds.
The team will use the AWS lab only as the fallback while the internal account and environment decision is pending.
The team will keep the workshop focused on the agent code, observability, and evaluation rather than mainstream deployment.
Unresolved Matters:
The final deployment account, AgentCore availability date, service ownership model, network and identity boundaries, and platform approval path remain open.
